even as all eyes are focused on the case  of george floyd and police misconduct in  minneapolis  there's another troubling issue that's  getting some well deserved attention  all the way to the white house a spike  in violent attacks against  asian americans across the country  an uptick in recent weeks in violent  attacks  against asian americans this  surveillance video capturing an alleged  assailant  in california violently shoving an 84  year old thai  american man to the ground he died of  his injuries days later  this surveillance footage shows a 71  year old asian grandmother violently  shoved to the ground  her purse stolen 52 year old woman  being pushed to the ground on roosevelt  avenue in flushing queens tuesday  a 91 year old man pushed in oakland's  chinatown  the 36 year old victim who is  asian-american walking when his attacker  runs up behind  and stabs him vicious hate crimes  against asian-americans  who've been attacked harassed blamed and  scapegoated  at this very moment so many of them  are fellow americans they're on the  front lines of this pandemic trying to  save lives  and still still they're forced to live  in fear  for their lives just walking down  streets in america  it's wrong it's un-american and it  must stop  we're joined now by john yang of the  group asian americans advancing justice  and mr yang thank you for being here  tonight  were you heartened to see president  biden address this issue  absolutely oftentimes asian americans  struggle for visibility  we are are thinking ourselves does  anyone see us especially  during this time when we've seen these  heightened attacks against our community  to have the president speak out so  forcefully and denounce these attacks  against our community  gives us hope there's still a long road  ahead but at least we know that  someone sees us during this time  and what do you think is at the heart of  this mr young  unfortunately there's a lot of things  going on and to be clear  we've seen this since back in january of  2020 when covet 19  first hit our shores in the united  states because of misinformation  disinformation  people have this association that  somehow asian americans are diseased or  special carriers of covet 19  and unfortunately we've seen that before  in history where we've had the so-called  yellow peril back in the late 1800s  or other issues where asian americans  are seen as the perpetual foreigner  so certainly number one that is a driver  as to  why there is this fear why there are  these attacks but the other thing we  need to remember is that  we are seeing a lot of economic  insecurity we  are seeing a lot of fear and whenever  people have fear  they look for someone to blame and  unfortunately in this case  the asian american community has become  a target  a question for you mr yang from rhonda  schwartz rhonda  john what if do you have any advice  if you are attacked or if perhaps you  see an attack like this going on what  should people do  sure and there's a lot of good things  that people can do and i appreciate that  question  first is it's important to report it  it's certainly  again it goes to this notion of  visibility that we've talked to  so our organization has a website called  stand against hatred dot org  where people can report heat incidents  so that we can document it we can tell  stories around it  the second thing i would encourage  people to consider is get what is called  bystander intervention training  so that allows a person if they see an  act of hate  and it doesn't have to be directed at  the asian american community but if they  see an act of hate or racism  they can do something about it now to be  clear i'm not asking people to be  superheroes and jump  in and stop something but there's  oftentimes little things that you can do  that makes that victim just feel a  little bit better and suffer a little  bit  less trauma in that moment  let me ask you this uh the role of  former president trump he continues to  refer to  kova 19 as the chinese virus he hasn't  been helpful has he  unfortunately he has absolutely not been  helpful and even just a few days ago  before president biden spoke  he referred to it again as the china  virus and certainly we know that anytime  language that is used like that it has  caused a spike in anti-asian racism  so it is clear that that is one of the  causes  clauses  the fact is that these words matter they  matter to making people  think about cloven 19 in a certain way  to cause people to blame a certain  community for clove at 19.  and that i think president biden had it  right that has to stop  that's un-american  and do you see it stopping since his  speech last week  well unfortunately it's going to be a  long journey and number one is  we hope that as people become more aware  of this issue  people start to respond to it react to  it in a good way  but i think the other thing we have to  recognize is that this isn't going to go  away  anytime soon you know the reality is we  do have  geopolitical tensions with the chinese  government and those are real and those  are justifiable  but anytime you have these types of  world tensions there's  a need or a desire to blame a particular  community  and so even after covet 19 passes or is  under control  this is still an issue this issue of  racism that we all  must work on together  all right john yang of the group asian  americans advancing justice  thank you so much for being here tonight  thank you very much  coming up next this week's winners and  losers in the media  see if you agree with the choices made  by the editors of mediaite  you're watching brian ross investigates  on the law on crime trial network
 

